Trimmed real data (ruling T1: GitHub download counts are open data).

source.json holds 27 release assets of 11 repositories, as the GitHub REST and
GraphQL APIs reported them on 2026-09-26: release tag, publish time,
prerelease flag, asset name, download count and asset creation time. Nothing
else is kept: no author, uploader, release notes or URLs, so no personal data.
Every other repository listed in config/sources/github_releases.toml answers
with no releases.

http/ and snapshot/ are generated from source.json by
`uv run python -m tests.collectors.github_releases.build_fixture`, through the
fake API in tests/collectors/github_releases/fakegithub.py: the response
bodies keep the API's field names but only the fields above (plus each
release's html_url, rebuilt from the repository and tag), and the GraphQL
cursors, the Link header's repository id and the response headers are
invented. expected.jsonl is the collector's parse of snapshot/.

Source: https://docs.github.com/en/rest/releases/releases and
https://docs.github.com/en/graphql (the repositories' public releases).
GitHub's Acceptable Use Policies allow collecting public, non-personal
information through the API. The download counts state no licence; they are
used under GitHub's Terms of Service. Tag and asset names are the
repositories' own; each font's licence is in its repository.
